From ea5551659032e9c13782e763603ceb501310ac7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EJ Campbell <ejc3@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:13:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] x86/kvm: Leave the async #PF reason for the fault it belongs
 to

A KVM guest panics with "Host injected async #PF in interrupt disabled
region" when an NMI arrives between the delivery of an async #PF and
the moment its handler reads the reason flags, and the NMI handler
itself takes a page fault. perf does exactly that when it samples with
user call chains: the sampling NMI reads user memory, and a read past
the top of the stack, or through a frame pointer that is not one,
faults. The nested fault calls __kvm_handle_async_pf(), reads and
clears the per-CPU reason flags of the outer async #PF, sees that the
NMI context has interrupts disabled, and panics.

The NMI entry code saves and restores CR2 for this nesting, but not the
async #PF reason. The race was described when async #PF was reworked
in 2020, with perf as the example, and left open.

Since that rework the guest does not set KVM_ASYNC_PF_SEND_ALWAYS, so
the host delivers async #PF only to user mode, and the reason flags
belong to a user-mode fault. Read them only for a fault from user mode.
A kernel-mode fault inside an NMI, #MC or #DB, which all enter through
irqentry_nmi_enter(), is a real fault and returns without touching the
flags, which the outer handler then finds intact once the NMI returns.
A kernel-mode fault outside those handlers only checks whether a reason
is pending, and keeps the "async #PF in kernel mode" panic for a host
that delivers one.

Reproduced in a 4-vCPU guest restored from a snapshot with its memory
served on demand through userfaultfd, so that reading it back takes
async #PFs, while perf samples hardware cycles with user call chains
and the reading loop's frame pointer points at an unmapped page: the
guest panicked about 20 seconds after boot in every run.
---
 arch/x86/kernel/kvm.c | 29 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/kvm.c b/arch/x86/kernel/kvm.c
index b67d7c5..ac566fd 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/kvm.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/kvm.c
@@ -255,11 +255,36 @@ noinstr u32 kvm_read_and_reset_apf_flags(void)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(kvm_read_and_reset_apf_flags);
 
+static __always_inline bool kvm_apf_reason_pending(void)
+{
+	return __this_cpu_read(async_pf_enabled) &&
+	       __this_cpu_read(apf_reason.flags);
+}
+
 noinstr bool __kvm_handle_async_pf(struct pt_regs *regs, u32 token)
 {
-	u32 flags = kvm_read_and_reset_apf_flags();
 	irqentry_state_t state;
+	u32 flags;
+
+	/*
+	 * The host delivers async #PF only to user mode, because the guest
+	 * does not enable KVM_ASYNC_PF_SEND_ALWAYS, so the reason flags belong
+	 * to a fault from user mode. A #PF from kernel mode can be nested in an
+	 * NMI (or #MC, or #DB) that arrived after an async #PF was delivered
+	 * and before its handler read the flags, for instance perf reading
+	 * user memory for a call chain. That fault is a real one: leave the
+	 * flags for the fault they belong to. Outside such a handler, pending
+	 * flags on a kernel-mode fault mean the host is broken.
+	 */
+	if (!user_mode(regs)) {
+		if (likely(in_nmi() || !kvm_apf_reason_pending()))
+			return false;
+		state = irqentry_enter(regs);
+		instrumentation_begin();
+		panic("Host injected async #PF in kernel mode\n");
+	}
 
+	flags = kvm_read_and_reset_apf_flags();
 	if (!flags)
 		return false;
 
@@ -275,8 +300,6 @@ noinstr bool __kvm_handle_async_pf(struct pt_regs *regs, u32 token)
 		panic("Host injected async #PF in interrupt disabled region\n");
 
 	if (flags & KVM_PV_REASON_PAGE_NOT_PRESENT) {
-		if (unlikely(!(user_mode(regs))))
-			panic("Host injected async #PF in kernel mode\n");
 		/* Page is swapped out by the host. */
 		kvm_async_pf_task_wait_schedule(token);
 	} else {
-- 
